00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:26.880
OK.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.280
Let's first define latency.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.200
It's essentially the delay
between request and response.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.000
But what's application latency?

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.320
You came here to this talk.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.870
The title is "Minimizing
App Latency."

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.590
OK, I think it's
just a made-up term.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.789
There are about 30,000
results on Google.

00:02:43.789 --> 00:02:45.330
So I'm going to
define it here today.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.970
So application latency,
I am proposing,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.360
is the delay between a
user's action and update.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.320
So this is essentially
a holistic UX thing.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.110
So the latency is the time
the user presses a button

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.429
or initiates an action and
when they see a result.

00:03:03.429 --> 00:03:05.970
So this is actually nothing to
do with networking or anything

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.386
like that, although
it is related.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.030
When does it matter?

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.430
A good rule of thumb is
that your most common cases

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:16.820
should be faster.

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:21.120
So given a blog service,
viewing a blog post

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.250
is the most frequent
action, maybe.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:28.000
A lot of people-- a lot of
blogs are not read very often.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.320
Going down to viewing
the comments for a blog.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.700
So maybe as you scroll
down, load those in later.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.950
Commenting on a blog post,
and publishing and editing,

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.862
so essentially the
write operations.

00:03:37.862 --> 00:03:39.820
And then finally, the
most infrequent operation

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.111
is creating a new blog,
although perhaps more blogs are

00:03:42.111 --> 00:03:44.650
created than posts are created.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.720
So that might be the wrong
thing to optimize for.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.430
There's an interesting anecdote
which I think was from Blogger.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.330
The blog creation process--

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:54.940
that is, creating
an entire new site--

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:58.630
was so fast that users thought
that something was wrong.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:01.670
They would click Create
New Blog, and boom,

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:02.620
it would just happen.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.930
And I think that left users
wondering whether something

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.920
really happened at all or not.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.910
So sometimes fast is not better.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.520
Optimize for user satisfaction
over everything else.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:20.019
So we might be able to change
from frequent to infrequent,

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:22.690
to insignificant to
significant, right?

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:24.430
Maybe significant
operations are slower

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.050
because the user is
committing something,

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.677
so maybe they expect you to
commit something back, right?

00:04:30.677 --> 00:04:32.260
So they expect
something to be slower.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.530
So the next thing I
want to talk about

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.664
is managing user expectations.

00:04:39.664 --> 00:04:41.580
When can you get away
with making things slow?

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.100
If you can't make
things fast, then

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.802
make the user understand
why things are slow.

00:04:46.802 --> 00:04:48.885
I think we can all understand
that re-architecting

00:04:48.885 --> 00:04:51.690
our applications in a
way to make things fast

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.672
is sometimes not possible.

00:04:53.672 --> 00:04:55.380
So when can we get
away with slow things?

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.724
We already talked about
more infrequent operations.

00:04:58.724 --> 00:05:00.390
Just telling the user
it's going to take

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.754
a while might be good enough.

00:05:02.754 --> 00:05:04.170
And more importantly,
telling them

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.210
why it's going to take a while.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.030
Giving users frequent
updates and indications

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:10.105
is also really good.

00:05:10.105 --> 00:05:11.730
Tell them why it's
happening and what's

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:13.570
happening under the hood.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.420
So an app that does
this really well

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.730
is just the BigQuery
user interface.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.980
There could be some
improvements, obviously.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.140
But what it does, when
you hit Run Query,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.509
it just has a timer, a
stopwatch, that goes up.

00:05:25.509 --> 00:05:27.300
And so this is giving
users some indication

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.160
that something is happening.

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.890
They don't really
know exactly what's

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:34.680
happening under the hood,
although once the job has

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.740
completed, you can see the
explanation for the query.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:40.340
And it tells you,
importantly, how many

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:41.480
gigabytes were processed.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.280
So it's telling you why this
operation took so long, right,

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.520
because we processed all
of these gigabytes of data.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:50.070
So this sets user expectations.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.570
They know they're
querying huge data sets,

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.990
but they're only
happening in 17 seconds,

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:57.930
whereas this operation,
obviously, something

00:05:57.930 --> 00:05:59.700
taking 17 seconds might
not be appropriate

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.437
for other applications.

00:06:01.437 --> 00:06:03.270
I'm thinking about game
servers or something

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:06.490
like that, where we're
talking tens of milliseconds

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:09.034
rather than tens of seconds.

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:10.950
So you have to consider
everything in context.

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:16.900
So breaking down, at a really
high level, application

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.250
latency, we can think of things,
so on the left of this slide,

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.330
would be the user
initiating an action,

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:29.140
and on the right, when
they see the actual update.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:32.260
We have any network requests,
which I think a lot of people

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:34.330
here are concerned
about, which consists

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.700
of latency of the
requests going out,

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:38.200
whatever's happening
on the server,

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.560
and maybe dependent
servers from there,

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:43.240
and then the time it
takes to come back.

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.580
And there may be some sort
of on-device processing

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:49.480
like storing the results in
a local cache or database,

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:55.030
and then whatever happens in
the UI, so reflows and repaints.

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.410
These are all part of
application latency.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:00.770
So this is what the
user experiences.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:03.400
So one trick that
you might know is

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.890
to sort of decouple these
things to reduce the perceived

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:07.400
latency.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:10.150
So maybe doing some
of this work upfront,

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:13.240
and sending off the network
request in the background.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.900
A really good example of this
is anything with a Like button

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:19.840
or a +1 button or
something like that.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.690
The UI updates instantaneously.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:26.320
The app confirms that the
user performed an action,

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.839
and the network request
happens in the background.

00:07:29.839 --> 00:07:31.880
So you might do some sort
of on-device processing

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:34.990
beforehand, like storing the
fact that the user initiated

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:37.900
this action, so that
you could possibly

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:44.350
retry the action in
future, or display

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:46.880
that the network request
failed, or something like that.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.510
And I'm actually
using this graphic.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:51.820
We're going to look
at each of these boxes

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:53.720
separately throughout the talk.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:57.164
So we're actually going to
start more from the UI side.

00:07:57.164 --> 00:07:58.830
We've been talking a
lot about UX stuff.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.785
But yes, we will be
talking about networking

00:08:00.785 --> 00:08:01.660
and things like that.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:09.920
So how do you know what the
slow part of your app is?

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:12.510
We need to figure out what's
slow and how to fix it

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.450
and what the problems are.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.340
Google Analytics does
this automatically

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.440
for web applications.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.430
This is actually a real
app that I've worked on

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.670
of anonymize the page URLs.

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:27.720
But it gives you a
really nice indication

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:31.230
of several important statistics,
in this case, the document

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:34.650
interactive time, which
is when the page can

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:35.637
be interacted with.

00:08:35.637 --> 00:08:37.470
So this is separate
from Dom content loaded,

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:41.340
if you're a web developer,
which I think is a poor metric.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:43.497
This is a better metric.

00:08:43.497 --> 00:08:45.330
So this is a really
simple way to figure out

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.538
which of your pages are slow.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.000
So once you've figured
out which pages are slow,

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.220
using the Chrome Developer
Console or the equivalent

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.475
for Android or iOS
is a really good way

00:08:58.475 --> 00:09:00.100
to figure out exactly
what's happening.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.130
So this is actually
a timeline graph

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:06.600
of Gmail, which is an incredibly
complicated application.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.120
So it shows you things
like what JavaScript

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.880
is running, what network
requests are happening,

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.790
and what paints and things
like that that are happening,

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.920
like compositors on the GPU,
things like that, so you

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:19.560
can figure out exactly
what's happening

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.100
inside your application.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.080
Detecting the document
interactive time and Dom

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:34.570
content loaded are
good, but even better

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:38.177
is getting actual
timings from your users.

00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:40.510
So you might have some sort
of lazy loading or something

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:41.740
like that.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:44.210
With Google Analytics,
you can send these timings

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:46.780
to Google Analytics,
and you'll be

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:50.590
able to slice and dice it like
any other events or triggers

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:53.160
or anything that happens
inside Analytics.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:56.830
So this is a really handy
way to customize your timings

00:09:56.830 --> 00:10:00.560
once you've figured out exactly
what components of your page

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.310
or application that
you want to figure out.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:09.310
So this is the same code
for Java, so on Android.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:12.550
And you'll end up
in the analytics

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:13.850
with a graph like this.

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:16.450
So this is a real app, again.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.820
So you get timing buckets.

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:20.750
The most interesting
thing, I think for me,

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:22.570
are the ones that
take the slowest.

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:25.300
I want to figure out
how many of my users

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.280
are going to become disengaged.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.620
So anything above-- I
think a good rule of thumb,

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:32.220
again, is about three seconds.

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.810
After three seconds, a user
is going to become disengaged.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:38.994
And we can figure out globally
where these users are.

00:10:38.994 --> 00:10:39.910
So I'm from Australia.

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:42.490
Things are really slow
in Australia, generally.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:45.940
And it's no different for
this particular application,

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.530
although I think these
statistics are faulty.

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:51.180
840 seconds is a long time.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:56.116
I don't think things
are that quite bad.

00:10:56.116 --> 00:10:56.770
But yeah.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:04.230
So yeah, going back, let's talk
about network requests now.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:08.910
Why are things so
slow from Australia?

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.040
OK, so a good first step,
if you haven't already,

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:13.740
is setting up monitoring
for your back end,

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:14.940
or your front end.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.740
Both are good.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.050
This is just the way
to add an uptime check

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:21.060
in Stackdriver monitoring.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.150
And after you do that, it'll
send a request every minute,

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:28.861
or however often you want, and
give you a graph like this.

00:11:28.861 --> 00:11:30.360
So it's important
to note that these

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.180
are actual timings
from Stackdriver,

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.430
not an average of all
of your user requests

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:36.960
or anything like that.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.690
So this is really good for
detecting if your app is up,

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.790
and getting a sense of
how long things are taking

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:45.240
on your back end, but
it's only measuring things

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:48.210
from the perspective of
the Stackdriver probers.

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:51.159
These are not actual
timings from your users,

00:11:51.159 --> 00:11:52.450
so it's really not that useful.

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:54.033
It's good for keeping
track of uptime,

00:11:54.033 --> 00:11:55.500
but not for identifying issues.

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:02.280
So how do we identify
issues using tracing?

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:04.420
And those user metrics
are really important too.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:10.590
So now we're sort of diving
into this remote processing,

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:12.420
like, whatever's
happening on your server.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:13.795
Why are things
happening so slow?

00:12:13.795 --> 00:12:16.930
Why are the requests
taking so long?

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:19.795
So Stackdriver Trace
is the way to do this.

00:12:19.795 --> 00:12:23.420
This is enabled by default
for App Engine standard,

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:26.980
and this app is actually
an App Engine standard app,

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:30.220
and you get this nice
dot plot graph showing

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:32.966
requests over time and
whichever ones were sampled

00:12:32.966 --> 00:12:34.090
and sort of where they are.

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:37.279
So on the y-axis is
the latency, and you

00:12:37.279 --> 00:12:39.070
can see some of the
requests are getting up

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:41.410
there over that sort
of three-second mark

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:44.844
where we're getting concerned.

00:12:44.844 --> 00:12:47.260
And generally, the slow ones
are the most interesting ones

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:48.789
to see, to look at.

00:12:48.789 --> 00:12:50.830
And you can actually click
on these specific dots

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:54.194
to look at the traces of
those specific requests.

00:12:54.194 --> 00:12:56.110
And there's also this
sweet auto-reload thing,

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:57.984
and you can see the
traces come through live.

00:13:00.474 --> 00:13:02.640
Another really interesting
view in Stackdriver Trace

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:06.180
is this latency profile.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:09.840
So the graph in
orange is this week,

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:11.400
and the blue is
the previous week.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:13.483
It's actually really
interesting that SAP has such

00:13:13.483 --> 00:13:15.660
a consistent latency profile.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:19.914
I'd expect these things to
change a fair bit week to week.

00:13:19.914 --> 00:13:20.810
But yeah.

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:23.910
This is a really great way
to see how many of your users

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:26.690
are in sort of each bucket
that you care about.

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:32.130
So there are some users
above that three-second mark,

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.770
and you'll be able to see
the percentage of people

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:38.220
that are becoming disengaged.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.170
The comparison is also
interesting to detect

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:44.840
regressions in your
application performance.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:48.450
So Stackdriver also
produces reports.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:51.410
It will provide sample
traces for each sort

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:54.050
of percentile buckets.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:57.206
So again, I think the most
important ones to look at

00:13:57.206 --> 00:13:58.080
are the slowest ones.

00:14:00.697 --> 00:14:01.780
So this is a single trace.

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:05.420
This is one of the slower ones
inside this particular app.

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:07.220
Running on App
Engine standard, you

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:11.360
can see it's doing a bunch of
memcache, data store requests,

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:14.700
and then sort of nothing happens
after the one-second mark,

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:15.200
right?

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:18.480
We have no idea what the
application is doing.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.790
So what happened there?

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:22.440
I don't know.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:24.231
I haven't instrumented
this particular app.

00:14:24.231 --> 00:14:28.500
But using the Stackdriver
API to provide custom spans--

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:33.150
so each of these particular
timings is called a span--

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:34.662
it's the way to do that.

00:14:34.662 --> 00:14:36.870
There's also Zipkin integration,
so if you're already

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:38.310
using Zipkin for traces.

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:39.370
Well, if you're using--

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:42.750
who here is tracing already?

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:44.506
OK, that's a pretty good amount.

00:14:44.506 --> 00:14:46.380
So if you're using a
tracing product already,

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:48.485
there might be an
integration with Stackdriver.

00:14:48.485 --> 00:14:52.380
With Zipkin, you can
send all of those traces

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:54.480
to look at them
in this great UI.

00:14:57.701 --> 00:14:58.200
But yeah.

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:00.630
So how do we mitigate
these slow requests?

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:04.860
So for example
here, maybe there's

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:08.930
some sort of processing event or
something like that happening.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:10.810
We don't know.

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:12.699
So how do you mitigate them?

00:15:12.699 --> 00:15:14.990
I think a really good way
to, and if you can afford to,

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:18.380
is to write these requests
to low-latency event

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:19.970
queues in other services.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:23.540
So I'm not going to teach you
how to create an event pipeline

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:28.160
or anything like that, but
what I'm essentially suggesting

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:30.440
here is to store these
payloads somewhere else

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:33.560
temporarily before you can
process and ingest them.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:37.697
So make those client
requests asynchronous.

00:15:37.697 --> 00:15:39.530
I've talked to a bunch
of people outside who

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:40.500
are using App Engine.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:41.875
Task queues are
a really good way

00:15:41.875 --> 00:15:44.150
of doing this, very
high performance.

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:46.580
You can put those
requests in there

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:49.730
and process them with
a push or pull queue

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:52.220
as you have capacity to do so.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:55.504
The rate that you do that might
rely on whatever underlying

00:15:55.504 --> 00:15:56.420
services you're using.

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:59.130
So if you're using
DataStore, for example,

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:02.600
you might only want to write
100 requests per second

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:04.880
or something like that.

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:07.910
Cloud Storage, Spanner, and
Pub/Sub are also really good.

00:16:07.910 --> 00:16:12.200
Spanner and GCS Cloud Storage
have multi-region modes,

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:15.620
which means that if your servers
are globally distributed,

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:19.690
you'll be able to write
into those quickly.

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:23.102
And REST support for task
queues is coming soon,

00:16:23.102 --> 00:16:25.310
so you'll be able to use
those outside of App Engine.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:33.640
So maybe we identified
a function or a handler

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:35.200
that's performing poorly.

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:42.600
I think the next thing to do
is to profile that function.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:46.550
So pprof is a open-source
tool from Google

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:50.124
for visualizing and analyzing
that profiling data.

00:16:50.124 --> 00:16:52.040
You get the profiling
data through other tools

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:56.700
like Linux's perf tool.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.300
And this is generally good
for things that are CPU-bound

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.160
or I/O-bound or
something like that,

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.390
and you can figure out
exactly what's happening.

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:06.380
So on the left is the output
from the pprof command line

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:09.680
tool, basically see
the top functions.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:12.710
You can dig in deeper
through different methods.

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:16.400
And on the right is a very
complicated graphical output

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:18.589
which shows you
basically call graphs

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:20.300
and how long things are taking.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:22.879
I think the larger boxes
are things that take longer,

00:17:22.879 --> 00:17:27.140
or where more CPU time is spent.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.990
And another really great
way to visualize this

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:31.910
is using a flame graph.

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:33.710
So the colors actually
don't mean anything.

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:37.670
I think that's where the name
"flame" comes from, though.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:43.490
Basically the width--
and the x-axis

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:46.250
isn't anything to do with
time or anything like that.

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:49.640
The wider the block,
the more often

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:55.100
it shows up in stack
traces for the profile.

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:58.190
And at the very top are the
things actually spending

00:17:58.190 --> 00:17:59.700
CPU time.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:03.320
So this is a good way to
identify potential things

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:03.910
to fix.

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:06.954
So in this one, I don't know,
optimizing text template

00:18:06.954 --> 00:18:08.870
or something like that,
maybe adding a caching

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:11.166
layer there is appropriate.

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:20.250
So after you've figured out a
particular functional method

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:23.430
that needs to be
improved, benchmarking

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:26.010
is a really good
way of doing this.

00:18:26.010 --> 00:18:27.540
I always say to profile first.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:30.972
Otherwise, you're kind of
benchmarking in the dark.

00:18:30.972 --> 00:18:33.180
You really want to figure
out what to benchmark first

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:35.810
and find problem areas.

00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:37.980
It's also really important
to replicate these

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:39.620
results in production.

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:41.520
So production
environments vary--

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:43.650
like, differ greatly
from your workstation,

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:45.762
in terms of their
performance characteristics.

00:18:45.762 --> 00:18:47.220
So there's things
like hypervisors.

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.553
There was an interesting
story, I think a couple of days

00:18:49.553 --> 00:18:52.260
ago, about the
gettimeofday syscall

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.780
taking a really long time on--

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:58.369
it wasn't GC, but
another cloud provider.

00:18:58.369 --> 00:19:00.660
So you might have problems
like that that only manifest

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:01.701
themselves in production.

00:19:01.701 --> 00:19:06.420
So you really need to run these
profiling and benchmarking

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:09.420
on your production environment.

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:11.910
Generally, these VMs have a
lot of raw processing power.

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:13.867
Obviously, it depends
how big your VM is,

00:19:13.867 --> 00:19:16.200
and there might be different
characteristics for writing

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.120
to disk or anything like that.

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:22.724
Like, if you have SSDs or
a network-attached disk

00:19:22.724 --> 00:19:24.390
or something like
that, your application

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:26.940
might perform quite differently.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:30.780
Another way your application
performance might change

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:31.650
is under load.

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:35.400
So it's really, really
important to do load testing.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:38.130
I worked on an application
a few years ago

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:40.470
that completely fell
apart under load.

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.570
It ran really quickly when
a small amount of load

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:45.690
was sent at it, but
once it was replicated

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:49.980
and out in production, you know,
many, many different clones

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:52.500
of the service, it
just didn't scale,

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:55.010
once actual load was sent at it.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:56.260
So this is a good thing to do.

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:04.354
OK, let's talk about
getting close to your users.

00:20:04.354 --> 00:20:06.270
We're going to talk about
network latency now,

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:11.370
basically, so the request
and response latency.

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:17.655
OK, so back to
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:20:17.655 --> 00:20:19.030
This is another
interesting plot.

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:21.360
The one I showed you
previously aggregated

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:25.620
all of the latency profiles from
all of Stackdriver's locations,

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:28.120
but this one splits
them out separately.

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:31.290
So you can see here the orange
line, which is generally

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.095
at the top, or the
pink one, which

00:20:33.095 --> 00:20:35.910
is Sao Paulo and
Singapore, respectively,

00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:36.830
are the slowest ones.

00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:38.580
Presumably that's
because this application

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:41.710
is hosted in the US or
something like that.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:43.170
So this is a really
good way to get

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:45.510
an idea of how your
application performs

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:48.710
across the globe from
your different users.

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:50.700
So you might not
actually care about this.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:53.760
I was talking to someone
who has an application only

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:54.940
for US users.

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:57.090
This is not going to
be useful for them.

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:59.520
But if your Google
Analytics tells you

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:03.240
that you have users across
the world, the poor sods

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:05.750
in Singapore or
Australia or whatever,

00:21:05.750 --> 00:21:08.220
the app is really slow for
them, and maybe you had no idea.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:10.325
But this is giving
you an indication.

00:21:14.416 --> 00:21:17.040
And if we zoom out this graph--
this is another production app,

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:18.600
by the way.

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:19.850
I won't say which.

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:22.380
But if you zoom out, you
also see these huge spikes

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.390
across the week.

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:26.850
I have no idea what
happened there,

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:29.070
but this is another
great way to figure out

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:31.200
when to dig in a little more.

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:33.810
It's interesting that this
application spiked only

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:35.210
in certain regions.

00:21:35.210 --> 00:21:39.510
Why is Singapore so
slow for over a day?

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:45.430
There's also a lot of variance
from the other regions there,

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:45.930
too.

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:48.270
So maybe there was an outage
or something like that.

00:21:52.010 --> 00:21:56.660
OK, so now you've figured
out where your app is slow.

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:59.440
The thing to do is to figure
out where your users are,

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:01.600
put things close
to them, whether it

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:08.160
be front-end servers so you can
serve out your content to them,

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:11.190
or back-ends so maybe
replicating, distributing

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:12.720
data that's important for them.

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:17.614
So for example, if you
have users in Asia,

00:22:17.614 --> 00:22:19.030
maybe you want--
you probably want

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:20.770
to store their data
closest to them

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:22.670
so that they can access
it really quickly.

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:25.880
Maybe also replicate it
across to other regions,

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:28.600
but it should be
available close to them.

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:33.070
So this might be something
like a read-only replica.

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:36.340
And you'll have a trade-off
here between lowering

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:39.560
latency and data consistency
across the world.

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:42.460
A write that happens
in Asia, it's

00:22:42.460 --> 00:22:45.460
only possible to make it to
the US 200 milliseconds later.

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:47.860
So if a user in the US is
trying to read from it,

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:50.240
they'll see something different.

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:51.760
You can't beat physics, right?

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:55.330
So there's trade-offs
to do there.

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:59.050
One really easy way that will be
available soon to mitigate this

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:02.412
is using Spanner, which will
have a multi-region database.

00:23:02.412 --> 00:23:04.120
So that was actually
demoed this morning,

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.070
the multi-region database.

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:09.870
So that will
hopefully make things

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:11.800
quick to access from any region.

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:18.870
OK, so once you have all these
servers across the world,

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:24.000
you need to use load balancing,
and the Cloud load balancer

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:25.830
gives you intelligent
routing based

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:27.460
on the location of the user.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:30.360
And so if the closest instances
don't have enough capacity,

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:35.700
then it will send the request to
the nearest region after that.

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:40.180
So you'll always be hitting the
closest servers to your user.

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:43.230
It also gives you things like
automatic logging, including

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:44.100
latency statistics.

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:46.350
So from the perspective of
the load balancer, how long

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:47.910
did things take?

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:48.870
So this is really good.

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:52.050
And it's a pretty
easy thing to enable.

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:59.130
And it also integrates
really well into Cloud CDN,

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:01.135
which is not really like
a traditional CDN where

00:24:01.135 --> 00:24:03.510
you give them all of your
assets and they distribute them

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:06.360
across the world.

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:09.540
But it uses Google's
globally-distributed edge

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:10.820
points--

00:24:10.820 --> 00:24:14.520
points of presence,
sorry, and they cache

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:18.540
HTTP traffic and requests
closest to your users.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:21.870
So caching things at the
edge of Google's network

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:25.112
provides faster delivery
of content while reducing

00:24:25.112 --> 00:24:25.695
serving costs.

00:24:25.695 --> 00:24:30.710
So these requests aren't
being sent across networks.

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:32.930
It's closest to your
users, fast, and cheap.

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:38.680
So these are the
points of presence.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:42.550
I think the blue dots,
the dark blue dots,

00:24:42.550 --> 00:24:46.370
and the blue lines
represent Google's network.

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:49.220
And there are many
peering partners as well.

00:24:49.220 --> 00:24:53.240
So it was mentioned in the
keynote this morning, actually.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.790
So the peering partners
mean that it's likely

00:24:55.790 --> 00:24:58.369
there's only one hop between
your users and the content

00:24:58.369 --> 00:24:59.660
that you need to serve to them.

00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:05.956
And this also means that
as much traffic as possible

00:25:05.956 --> 00:25:07.330
is sent through
Google's network.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:09.300
So even if that
request does need

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:12.300
to go from Asia to
America, it's happening

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:14.890
over Google's network.

00:25:14.890 --> 00:25:18.720
You've heard all about the
excellence and performance

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:20.889
of the network this morning.

00:25:20.889 --> 00:25:22.680
But yeah, this is a
really great way to go.

00:25:25.670 --> 00:25:28.750
So it's really easy to enable.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:31.780
On your load balancer config,
you just hit a check box.

00:25:31.780 --> 00:25:33.626
I don't know why
you wouldn't do it.

00:25:33.626 --> 00:25:35.500
Maybe if you're concerned
about content being

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:37.000
stored in different places.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:40.764
But just hit Enable or
run this GCloud command,

00:25:40.764 --> 00:25:41.680
and it will just work.

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:48.420
And by "just work"
I mean you need

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:50.070
to do a little bit of work.

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:53.870
Not all HTTP responses
are cachable.

00:25:53.870 --> 00:25:56.650
It will only cache ones
that meet these criteria.

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:59.970
So in short, there's a bunch
more inside the documentation,

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:02.310
but basically it needs
to be a GET request.

00:26:02.310 --> 00:26:04.140
You need to set cache headers.

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:09.620
It needs to be, like, an OK
status code, essentially.

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:12.150
And you need to set
a content length.

00:26:12.150 --> 00:26:14.520
And it also can't be a
large request or anything

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:15.830
with a set cookie header.

00:26:15.830 --> 00:26:19.080
So this is good for
static assets that

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:20.970
is going to be common
across multiple users,

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:22.740
doesn't have any
personalized information

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:23.656
or anything like that.

00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:30.020
Another really cool
thing you can do

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:34.190
is set URL maps inside
the load balancer.

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:39.320
You can basically say send
requests to my back-ends,

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:43.820
and send static requests to my
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:46.490
And obviously, all of that
gets monitored and logged,

00:26:46.490 --> 00:26:49.545
and it's fronted by Cloud CDN.

00:26:49.545 --> 00:26:51.170
So what you need to
do there, if you're

00:26:51.170 --> 00:26:54.260
backing URL paths
with Cloud Storage,

00:26:54.260 --> 00:26:58.280
is to set the cache settings
on your Cloud Storage bucket.

00:26:58.280 --> 00:27:03.140
Basically you want to say,
cache all of these assets.

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:04.790
They're not going to
change very often.

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:09.500
Maybe you don't care if
they get cached forever.

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:12.500
You can be clever about
changing URL paths or something

00:27:12.500 --> 00:27:15.050
like that if you need
to flush the caches.

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:18.600
There's also ways to flush
the caches at the edge nodes.

00:27:18.600 --> 00:27:21.510
That's in the
documentation as well.

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:25.100
So this should reduce
the latency, particularly

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:27.200
for users that
aren't anywhere near

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.340
the zones of your back ends.

00:27:34.670 --> 00:27:38.060
OK, so I'm starting to wrap up.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:42.080
We've looked at various
aspects of application latency

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:46.900
or perceived
performance, comprising

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:51.200
the performance of these network
requests, whatever is happening

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:54.620
on the device, including UI
updates, whether you can do

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:59.060
any tricks to send
things asynchronously,

00:27:59.060 --> 00:28:02.036
both on the front
end and the back end,

00:28:02.036 --> 00:28:04.660
and getting close to your users
to reduce that network latency.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:13.520
OK, but importantly,
make it fast enough.

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:15.830
Remember that story
about creating blogs.

00:28:15.830 --> 00:28:18.680
Don't make it too fast, because
your users might get freaked

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:20.330
out.

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:22.434
Take a completely
holistic approach.

00:28:22.434 --> 00:28:24.350
You might have a front-end
and back-end teams.

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:26.450
Make sure they're
working together

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:30.110
to reduce the apparent
latency for your users.

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:34.250
Measure their actual latency
from your users' devices,

00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:35.670
and test from real devices.

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:37.790
You can use things
like Charles Proxy

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:43.550
to introduce packet loss and
latency and load bandwidth.

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:48.730
There's also ways on the Android
and iOS simulators to do this.

00:28:48.730 --> 00:28:51.650
Collect timings and metrics
from your actual users,

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:54.740
and instrument your back ends
with tracing so you can figure

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:56.930
out exactly what's going on.

00:28:56.930 --> 00:28:58.810
Consider your user's
location, device

00:28:58.810 --> 00:29:00.120
and networking capability.

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:03.290
So again, simulating packet
loss is a really good thing.

00:29:03.290 --> 00:29:07.040
Figure out how your app
performs in poor conditions,

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:10.760
and use global load
balancing and CDNs.

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:15.020
That's a really low-risk
way of making things faster.

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:17.350
But above all else, focus
on user satisfaction.

00:29:17.350 --> 00:29:19.100
If your users are
happy, then you probably

00:29:19.100 --> 00:29:20.960
don't have to do
anything, right?

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:22.802
Making things faster,
you have to think

00:29:22.802 --> 00:29:25.010
about what impact that's
going to have on your users.

00:29:25.010 --> 00:29:26.511
Maybe you can better
spend that time

00:29:26.511 --> 00:29:28.176
that you would have
spent on performance

00:29:28.176 --> 00:29:30.170
working on a feature
or something like that.

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:33.520
If you have the luxury to have
multiple performance engineers

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.890
or something like that,
then this is great.

00:29:35.890 --> 00:29:36.890
Just keep investigating.

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:41.690
Figure out where things are
coming from, and mitigate them.

00:29:41.690 --> 00:29:43.130
And that's it.

00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:47.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

